# Line endings are normalized to LF on every machine. This is load-bearing, not cosmetic.
#
# `shadcn build` embeds each item's SOURCE as a JSON string inside public/r/<item>.json. Every tracked
# file here is already LF in the index, but on Windows core.autocrlf checks those blobs out as CRLF, so a
# build there bakes \r\n INTO the embedded content string. Measured 2026-07-29: 11 of 15 artifacts carried
# Windows-era CRLF while the 4 built on Linux did not — so the same registry handed consumers CRLF for
# `truncate` and LF for `data-table`, and every machine switch rewrote those 11 files. `eol=lf` makes the
# build byte-identical wherever it runs, which is what lets CI gate on `git diff --exit-code public/r`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts ship as registry items into other repos and run inside containers — CRLF breaks them.
*.sh text eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
